<https://w3id.org/eudpp#EnergyConsumption> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://w3id.org/eudpp#ResourceConsumption> .
<https://w3id.org/eudpp#EnergyConsumption> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Energy consumption of the product.\n\nThis class description needs to be extended/specialised according to the corresponding delegated acts or/and industry standards."@en .
<https://w3id.org/eudpp#EnergyConsumption> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Energy consumption"@en .
# 
# https://w3id.org/eudpp#EnvironmentalEmission
<https://w3id.org/eudpp#EnvironmentalEmission> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://w3id.org/eudpp#EnvironmentalEmission> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://w3id.org/eudpp#EnvironmentalPollution> .
<https://w3id.org/eudpp#EnvironmentalEmission> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Emission is the act or amount of sending out gas, heat, light, etc. It can also refer to harmful substances that are released into the environment, especially carbon dioxide.\n\n\nThis class description needs to be extended/specialised according to the corresponding delegated acts or/and industry standards."@en .
<https://w3id.org/eudpp#EnvironmentalEmission> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Environmental emission"@en .
<https://w3id.org/eudpp#EnvironmentalEmission> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#seeAlso> <https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/emission> .
# 
# https://w3id.org/eudpp#EnvironmentalFootprint
<https://w3id.org/eudpp#EnvironmentalFootprint> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://w3id.org/eudpp#EnvironmentalFootprint> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://w3id.org/eudpp#QuantitativeProperty> .
<https://w3id.org/eudpp#EnvironmentalFootprint> <http://purl.org/dc/terms/references> "ESPR Art 2 (24)"@en .
<https://w3id.org/eudpp#EnvironmentalFootprint> <http://purl.org/dc/terms/source> "https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2024/1781/oj" .
<https://w3id.org/eudpp#EnvironmentalFootprint> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Environmental footprint\nmeans a quantification of the environmental impacts resulting from a product throughout its life\ncycle, whether in relation to a single environmental impact category or an aggregated set of impact\ncategories based on the Product Environmental Footprint method established by Recommendation\n(EU) 2 021/2279 or other scientific methods developed by international organisations, widely tested\nin collaboration with different industry sectors and adopted or implemented by the Commission in\nother Union law. (ESPR Art 2 (24)).\n\nProduct parameters list ESPR Annex I\n(m) The environmental footprint of the product, expressed as a quantification;\n\nThis class description needs to be extended/specialised according to the corresponding delegated acts or/and industry standards."@en .
<https://w3id.org/eudpp#EnvironmentalFootprint> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Environmental footprint"@en .
# 
# https://w3id.org/eudpp#EnvironmentalPollution
<https://w3id.org/eudpp#EnvironmentalPollution> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://w3id.org/eudpp#EnvironmentalPollution> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://w3id.org/eudpp#QuantitativeProperty> .
<https://w3id.org/eudpp#EnvironmentalPollution> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Environmental pollution is the addition of any substance or form of energy to the environment at a rate faster than it can be dispersed or safely stored.\n\nThis class description needs to be extended/specialised according to the corresponding delegated acts or/and industry standards."@en .
<https://w3id.org/eudpp#EnvironmentalPollution> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Environmental pollution"@en .
<https://w3id.org/eudpp#EnvironmentalPollution> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#seeAlso> <https://www.britannica.com/science/pollution-environment> .
